From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 20:34:49 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: compile emulated stages without a thread limit

A compute pipeline built with a small maxTotalThreadsPerThreadgroup
miscompiles a large kernel. A value that every thread of the dispatch
computes identically is correct for the first eight threads of each SIMD
group and zero for the rest: a texture handle loaded from an argument buffer
samples zero, and a patch vertex loaded from the tessellation control output
reads zero, while the memory being read is intact. Which kernels are affected
depends on how many values are live at once, so dropping a texture lookup,
storing each result as soon as it is produced, or any unrelated change to the
emitted code moves the failure to another shader instead of removing it. The
same MSL compiles correctly once the limit is raised or left unset.

The stages a graphics pipeline emulates in compute inline the whole
application shader, and their threadgroup size is a driver constant rather
than something the application asked for, so those are exactly the pipelines
that promise Metal a small threadgroup for a large kernel. Emulated
tessellation evaluation loses a sampled texel of a dynamically indexed
sampler array, and an emulated evaluation stage feeding a passthrough
geometry shader reads zeroed patch vertices and rasterizes a different image
than the hardware post-tessellation path.

Compile those pipelines without a thread limit, and record it as
KK_WORKAROUND_16 so it can be disabled. Application compute shaders keep the
threadgroup size they declare, which is the size Metal has to honour.

Add two KosmicKrisp tests: one draws isolines through a tessellation
evaluation shader that indexes a sampler array with four indices read from
its input records, and one renders all three tessellation domains with and
without a passthrough geometry shader and compares the images.
